cros_ec: poll the EC event FIFO when IRQ/notify never fires (per-device)

On the Google Pixel Slate (nocturne, x86 LPC Chrome EC) the EC never
delivers MKBP events to the AP. Confirmed on-device by three independent
measurements, all with the EC's own console proving the events happen:

  - EC console logs every button press AND "MKBP common FIFO depth 16
    reached" (the EC's outgoing event FIFO overflowing), yet:
  - the hardware IRQ (chromeos-ec, IRQ 83) shows a total count of 1 for the
    whole boot -- it fires once (the boot-time unlock drain in
    cros_ec_register) and never again;
  - /sys/firmware/acpi/interrupts: no GPE and no SCI movement on button press;
  - /sys/kernel/debug/wakeup_sources: zero movement on an isolated 5-press
    test.

The DSDT confirms the button ACPI node (GOOG0007, "EC MKBP Device") has no
_PRW/_CRS/interrupt resource of its own -- only the parent GOOG0004 does, and
its wake GPE (gpe70) is disabled. So neither the threaded IRQ path
(cros_ec.c) nor the ACPI-notify path (cros_ec_lpc.c, Notify 0x80 on GOOG0004)
ever drains the FIFO after boot. The result: volume buttons, the sensor FIFO
(auto-rotate), and lid-angle are all permanently silent.

The synchronous EC command path is unaffected -- cros_ec_get_next_event()
drains the FIFO fine on demand, which is exactly what the existing boot-time
and resume-time drains rely on. This patch adds an optional periodic drain
as a fallback for boards (or specific EC instances on a board) whose async
delivery is dead.

REVISION (per-device, not board-wide): the original version of this patch
decided whether to poll ONCE per board via a DMI match, applied uniformly to
every cros_ec_device instance. The Pixel Slate exposes more than one EC --
the main EC over LPC (the one actually affected above) and a separate FPMCU
over SPI, whose own IRQ has always worked fine. The board-wide flag polled
the FPMCU too, purely because it shares a DMI match with the LPC EC, which
is pure overhead on an EC that was never broken. It also meant a firmware
fix restoring the LPC EC's own IRQ (MrChromebox 2609.0 does this on this
board) couldn't make the LPC EC stop polling either, since the decision
never looked at that EC's own IRQ state in the first place.

Fixed by deciding per ec_dev instead: in auto mode (the default), poll THIS
EC only if the board is DMI-matched AND this EC's own transport ended up
with no usable IRQ (ec_dev->irq <= 0, already known by the time
cros_ec_register() reaches this decision). An EC that already has a working
IRQ -- the FPMCU always, and now the LPC EC too on fixed firmware -- is left
alone regardless of what any other EC on the same board needs. The module
param (ec_event_poll_ms) is still a hard override in either direction,
applied to every EC instance, for anyone who needs to force it.

Not yet hardware-tested with this revision; compile-verified clean against
v7.2.7. Prior board-wide version was hardware-confirmed (buttons/auto-rotate
restored) on 7.1.11-BobZKernel-pixel-slate.

Signed-off-by: BobZKernel <bobgenaw420@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec.c b/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec.c
--- a/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec.c
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/cleanup.h>
+#include <linux/dmi.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/of_platform.h>
@@ -18,6 +19,7 @@
 #include <linux/platform_data/cros_ec_proto.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/suspend.h>
+#include <linux/workqueue.h>
 
 #include "cros_ec.h"
 
@@ -150,6 +152,88 @@
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(cros_ec_irq_thread);
 
+/*
+ * BobZKernel: EC event-FIFO polling fallback.
+ *
+ * On some boards (confirmed: Google Nocturne / Pixel Slate) an EC's event
+ * IRQ/notify never fires: the hardware IRQ fires once at boot and never
+ * again, and no ACPI notify reaches the driver either. That EC's outgoing
+ * event FIFO then fills and overflows while nothing drains it, so volume
+ * buttons, the sensor FIFO (auto-rotate), and lid-angle all go silent. The
+ * synchronous command path is unaffected -- cros_ec_get_next_event() drains
+ * fine on demand -- so we just call the same drain periodically as a fallback.
+ *
+ * Decided PER cros_ec_device, not per board: a board can expose more than one
+ * EC (Nocturne has both the main EC over LPC and an FPMCU over SPI), and a
+ * firmware fix can restore one transport's IRQ while another's is still dead
+ * -- or always worked fine, e.g. the FPMCU's IRQ has never been the broken
+ * one here, so it was needlessly polled too under a single board-wide flag.
+ *
+ * Disabled by default; auto-enabled per-device when (DMI-matched board) AND
+ * (this ec_dev ended up with no usable IRQ). Overridable via the module
+ * param, which still applies module-wide to every ec_dev instance.
+ * ec_event_poll_ms: <0 = auto (per-device), 0 = off, >0 = poll period in ms.
+ */
+#define CROS_EC_EVENT_POLL_DEFAULT_MS	40
+
+static int ec_event_poll_ms = -1;
+module_param(ec_event_poll_ms, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(ec_event_poll_ms,
+	"Poll the EC event FIFO every N ms as a fallback when an EC's event IRQ/notify never fires (-1=auto per-device, 0=off, >0=force on for every EC at period ms)");
+
+static const struct dmi_system_id cros_ec_event_poll_dmi[] = {
+	{
+		/* Google Pixel Slate (Nocturne): some EC event IRQ/notify never fires */
+		.matches = {
+			DMI_MATCH(DMI_SYS_VENDOR, "Google"),
+			DMI_MATCH(DMI_PRODUCT_NAME, "Nocturne"),
+		},
+	},
+	{ }
+};
+
+/*
+ * Resolve this ec_dev's poll period. The module param is a hard override in
+ * either direction (0 = never poll any EC, >0 = always poll every EC at that
+ * period) for anyone who needs to force it. The default (-1, auto) only
+ * polls THIS ec_dev if the board is DMI-matched AND this EC's own transport
+ * ended up with no usable IRQ (ec_dev->irq is set from cros_ec_register()'s
+ * caller before this runs) -- an EC that already has a working IRQ is left
+ * alone regardless of what any other EC on the same board needs.
+ */
+static int cros_ec_event_poll_period(struct cros_ec_device *ec_dev)
+{
+	if (ec_event_poll_ms == 0)
+		return 0;
+	if (ec_event_poll_ms > 0)
+		return ec_event_poll_ms;
+
+	if (ec_dev->irq > 0)
+		return 0;
+
+	return dmi_check_system(cros_ec_event_poll_dmi) ?
+		CROS_EC_EVENT_POLL_DEFAULT_MS : 0;
+}
+
+static void cros_ec_event_poll(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct cros_ec_device *ec_dev = container_of(to_delayed_work(work),
+						     struct cros_ec_device,
+						     event_poll_work);
+	bool ec_has_more_events;
+
+	if (ec_dev->suspended)
+		return;
+
+	do {
+		ec_has_more_events = cros_ec_handle_event(ec_dev);
+	} while (ec_has_more_events);
+
+	if (ec_dev->event_poll_ms > 0 && !ec_dev->suspended)
+		schedule_delayed_work(&ec_dev->event_poll_work,
+			msecs_to_jiffies(ec_dev->event_poll_ms));
+}
+
 static int cros_ec_sleep_event(struct cros_ec_device *ec_dev, u8 sleep_event)
 {
 	int ret;
@@ -243,6 +327,9 @@
 		goto exit;
 	}
 
+	/* BobZKernel: init the FIFO-poll fallback work (may stay unscheduled). */
+	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&ec_dev->event_poll_work, cros_ec_event_poll);
+
 	if (ec_dev->irq > 0) {
 		err = devm_request_threaded_irq(dev, ec_dev->irq,
 						cros_ec_irq_handler,
@@ -329,6 +416,20 @@
 	if (ec_dev->mkbp_event_supported)
 		cros_ec_irq_thread(0, ec_dev);
 
+	/*
+	 * BobZKernel: on boards whose EC event IRQ/notify never fires, poll the
+	 * event FIFO so buttons / sensors / lid keep working (see cros_ec_event_poll).
+	 */
+	if (ec_dev->mkbp_event_supported) {
+		ec_dev->event_poll_ms = cros_ec_event_poll_period(ec_dev);
+		if (ec_dev->event_poll_ms > 0) {
+			dev_info(dev, "EC event IRQ/notify unreliable on this board; polling FIFO every %d ms\n",
+				 ec_dev->event_poll_ms);
+			schedule_delayed_work(&ec_dev->event_poll_work,
+				msecs_to_jiffies(ec_dev->event_poll_ms));
+		}
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 exit:
 	platform_device_unregister(ec_dev->ec);
@@ -350,6 +451,9 @@
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &ec_dev->lock)
 		ec_dev->registered = false;
 
+	/* BobZKernel: stop the FIFO-poll fallback (no-op if never scheduled). */
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&ec_dev->event_poll_work);
+
 	if (ec_dev->mkbp_event_supported)
 		blocking_notifier_chain_unregister(&ec_dev->event_notifier,
 						   &ec_dev->notifier_ready);
@@ -399,6 +503,9 @@
 
 	disable_irq(ec_dev->irq);
 	ec_dev->suspended = true;
+
+	/* BobZKernel: pause FIFO polling across suspend (no EC cmds while down). */
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&ec_dev->event_poll_work);
 }
 
 /**
@@ -486,6 +593,11 @@
 
 	if (ec_dev->wake_enabled)
 		disable_irq_wake(ec_dev->irq);
+
+	/* BobZKernel: resume FIFO polling if it was enabled for this board. */
+	if (ec_dev->mkbp_event_supported && ec_dev->event_poll_ms > 0)
+		schedule_delayed_work(&ec_dev->event_poll_work,
+			msecs_to_jiffies(ec_dev->event_poll_ms));
 }
 
 /**
diff --git a/include/linux/platform_data/cros_ec_proto.h b/include/linux/platform_data/cros_ec_proto.h
--- a/include/linux/platform_data/cros_ec_proto.h
+++ b/include/linux/platform_data/cros_ec_proto.h
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include <linux/lockdep_types.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/notifier.h>
+#include <linux/workqueue.h>
 
 #include <linux/platform_data/cros_ec_commands.h>
 
@@ -206,6 +207,18 @@
 	ktime_t last_event_time;
 	struct notifier_block notifier_ready;
 
+	/*
+	 * BobZKernel: fallback poll of the EC event FIFO for an EC whose event
+	 * IRQ/ACPI-notify never fires (e.g. Google Nocturne / Pixel Slate's
+	 * main EC - its FPMCU, a separate cros_ec_device, has always had a
+	 * working IRQ and is unaffected). Decided per-device: see
+	 * cros_ec_event_poll_period() in cros_ec.c. event_poll_ms is the
+	 * resolved period for THIS ec_dev (0 = not polling); event_poll_work
+	 * is unused (never scheduled) when it's 0.
+	 */
+	struct delayed_work event_poll_work;
+	int event_poll_ms;
+
 	/* The platform devices used by the mfd driver */
 	struct platform_device *ec;
 	struct platform_device *pd;
